<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Eurotas</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Evrotas</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Eurotas is the main river of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laconia">Laconia</a> in the southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnese">Peloponnese</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, flowing past <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a> into the Laconian Gulf. Its fertile valley was the heart of the Mycenaean kingdom remembered as that of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/menelaus">Menelaus</a> and later of the Spartan state. In antiquity it was linked with many myths and cults, among them those of Helen.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/eurotas.jpg" alt="Eurotas" width="320" height="263" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: vaggelis vlahos · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Eurotas_evlahos.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Evrotas is the main river of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laconia">Laconia</a> and one of the major rivers of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnese">Peloponnese</a>. It is 82 kilometres long and drains a basin of 2,239 square kilometres. Its springs lie just northwest of the border between Laconia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arcadia">Arcadia</a>, near Skortsinos, and it is fed by tributaries from Mount Taygetos on the west and Mount Parnon on the east. The valley lies in a rift. Analysis of bore holes shows that it was a lake in the Pliocene. In classical times ancient authors described it as swampy but very fertile, and it was used mainly for fruit trees, especially olives. Earlier names of the river were Bomycas and Himeras, and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> it was called Iri.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Greek legend made Lelex, king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leleges">Leleges</a>, the ancestor of the peoples of the valley, and his son or grandson Eurotas the last of the line. Eurotas had a daughter, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a>, who married an outsider, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laconia">Lacedaemon</a>. The name Lacedaemon was probably that of the Mycenaean state. A drowned town off Pounta and the island of Pavlopetri, surveyed from 1968 and again in 2009, was occupied from the late <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a> into Byzantine times and was mainly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/early-bronze-age">Early Bronze Age</a>.</p><p>On the left bank, opposite <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a>, a ridge holds the Menelaon. A survey found that the whole ridge had been covered by a walled Mycenaean town of the late <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a>. Excavators concluded that in the later fifteenth century BCE it was the principal settlement of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laconia">Laconia</a>, and the dedications to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/menelaus">Menelaus</a> and Helen found there from the Archaic period identify it with the Homeric Sparta. Classical Sparta lay on the west bank about halfway down the valley. The Spartans made little use of the left bank, and the main traffic went south to the port of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gytheio">Gytheio</a>. Pausanias believed that the Eurotas and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alpheios">Alpheios</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arcadia">Arcadia</a> shared a source and were connected underground.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Much of the water of the Evrotas is drawn off for irrigation, mainly of citrus crops, and the river is almost dry in summer, though it floods in winter. The plain of Elos near the mouth is particularly fertile. The final 10 kilometres of the river run through alluvial soil that did not exist in antiquity, and the old coastline lay farther inland. Modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laconia">Laconia</a> covers the valley, and its seat is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a>, as in ancient times.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Evrotas (river)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Sparta lay in the valley of the <b>Eurotas River</b> in Laconia, in the south-eastern Peloponnese, between the Taygetus and Parnon mountain ranges.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sparta">Sparta · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In the <b>Eurotas</b> valley of Laconia, Mycenaean elites appear from c. 1500 BCE, as shown by the rich tholos tomb of Vapheio with its gold cups.</blockquote><a href="/state/mycenaean-kingdom-of-laconia">Mycenaean Kingdom of Laconia</a></li></ul></section>
